► **ANSWER**

## Analysis

The root cause of all 9 failures is a single bug in `do_mkdir_p`: the buffer `buf` is allocated with `malloc` (uninitialized) and the code writes `buf[i] = '\0'` expecting the rest to be a copy of `path`, but no copy was ever made. Additionally, because `buf` is mutated cumulatively (each slash position is set to NUL and never restored), even a proper initial copy would be corrupted by subsequent iterations.

## Fix

In `do_mkdir_p`, in the intermediate-component loop, replace:

```c
        buf[i] = '\0';
```

with:

```c
        memcpy(buf, path, i);
        buf[i] = '\0';
```

This ensures each iteration independently copies the correct prefix `path[0..i-1]` into `buf` before null-terminating at position `i`, so `check_path(buf)` and `mkdir(buf, 0777)` always see the intended prefix string.

## Edits to apply

In `src/c_mkdir/c_mkdir.c`, inside `do_mkdir_p`, find this line in the for-loop body:

```c
        buf[i] = '\0';
```

and replace it with these two lines:

```c
        memcpy(buf, path, i);
        buf[i] = '\0';
```

## Summary

| What was failing | Why |
|---|---|
| All nested `-p` cases (`a/b/c/d`, `x/y/z`, deep paths, dot-segments, symlink parents) | `buf` was uninitialized `malloc` memory; `check_path(buf)` read garbage, returned -1 (ENOENT), then `mkdir(buf, 0777)` also failed with ENOENT because the "path" was random bytes. Even where `check_path` happened to succeed, the cumulative NUL writes corrupted later prefixes. |

| Change | Effect |
|---|---|
| `memcpy(buf, path, i); buf[i] = '\0';` | Each loop iteration gets a correct C-string of the prefix to check/create, independent of prior iterations. Fixes all 9 failing cases. |

